Time Limit: 1 sec
Memory: 256 MB

Description
Alice and Bob have a keyboard that can type lowercase English alphabets. The keyboard also has a backspace key. When the backspace key is pressed, the last character from the string is removed, and if the string is empty, nothing happens.

Alice and Bob have both typed a string on their respective computers with this keyboard. When they pressed the backspace key, `#` was inserted into the string.

They want to know whether their strings are equal when typed into a text editor.

For example, when the string `aab#a` is typed into a text editor, we obtain `aaa`.

Input Format
Implement the `StringCompare` function that takes in the two strings `s` and `t`.

Output Format
Return `true` if the two strings are equal and `false` otherwise. For the provided template, output **Yes** if equal and **No** otherwise.

Constraints
1 ≤ |s| ≤ 100000
1 ≤ |t| ≤ 100000

Sample Input 1

```text
a##b
#a#b
```

Sample Output 1

```text
Yes
```

Sample Input 2

```text
a##b
#a#c
```

Sample Output 2

```text
No
```

Note
For the first sample, processing `s = a##b` results in `b` and processing `t = #a#b` also results in `b`. Hence they are equal.

For the second sample, processing `s = a##b` results in `b` while processing `t = #a#c` results in `c`. They are not equal.



#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;


bool StringCompare(string s, string t) {
	int n = s.size();
    int m = t.size();

    string ss = "";
    string tt = "";
    
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        if(s[i] != '#')
        {
            ss.push_back(s[i]);
        }
        else
        {
            if(!ss.empty())
            {
                ss.pop_back();
            }
        }
    }

     for(int i = 0; i < m; i++)
    {
        if(t[i] != '#')
        {
            tt.push_back(t[i]);
        }
        else
        {
            if(!tt.empty())
            {
                tt.pop_back();
            }
        }
    }

    return ss == tt;
}


int main() {
	ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
	cin.tie(NULL);
	string s, t;
	cin >> s >> t;
	if (StringCompare(s, t))
		cout << "Yes";
	else
		cout << "No";
}
